-- title:
--   A ramp function of $a$ acts as a unit on the cut-down $(a-v)_+$
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be a C*-algebra (not necessarily unital). For real numbers $l<u$ define the ramp function
--
--   $$
--   \rho_{l,u}(t)=\min\Big(1,\ \frac{\max(t-l,0)}{u-l}\Big),
--   $$
--
--   which vanishes for $t\le l$, increases linearly on $[l,u]$ and equals $1$ for $t\ge u$. For self-adjoint $a\in A$ write $\rho_{l,u}(a)$ for the element obtained by continuous functional calculus, and $(a-v)_+=f_v(a)$ with $f_v(t)=\max(t-v,0)$.
--
--   **Theorem.** Let $a\in A$ with $a\ge0$, and let $l,u,v\in\mathbb{R}$ with $0\le l<u\le v$. Then
--
--   $$
--   \rho_{l,u}(a)\,(a-v)_+=(a-v)_+.
--   $$
--
--   Thus the positive contraction $\rho_{l,u}(a)$ acts as a unit on the part of $a$ above level $u$; consequently it also fixes, from the left, every element of the hereditary subalgebra generated by $(a-v)_+$. For example, $\rho_{3/32,1/8}(X)$ fixes $(X-\tfrac18)_+$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $\rho_{l,u}(a)$ is `ramp a l u = cfcₙ (rampFn l u) a` and $(a-v)_+$ is `cut a v`. For this lemma the Lean statement equips $A$ with an arbitrary partial order (the star-ordered-ring assumption is omitted), and the hypothesis $0\le a$ is not used by the proof: the identity holds for every self-adjoint $a$, and trivially for non-self-adjoint $a$ because Mathlib's real functional calculus returns $0$ there. The hypothesis $0\le l$ (hence $v\ge0$) ensures that both functions vanish at $0$, as the non-unital functional calculus requires.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/OAI/Analysis/CStarAlgebra/PureInfiniteness/Cutoffs.lean#L194-L207

